  "description": "Nested Subqueries (1)",
  "code": "SELECT DISTINCT customer_name \n     FROM borrower\n     WHERE customer_name IN \n          (SELECT customer_name \n           FROM depositor);",
  "line_count": 5,
  "has_explanations": true,
  "lines": [
    {
      "line_no": 1,
      "code": "SELECT DISTINCT customer_name ",
      "explanation": "The subquery covered by round parenthesis is calculated at first and then using set membership test, the results are compared with main query results. <Br>By specifying DISTINCT, duplications in customer_name are eliminated."
    },
    {
      "line_no": 2,
      "code": "     FROM borrower",
      "explanation": ""
    },
    {
      "line_no": 3,
      "code": "     WHERE customer_name IN ",
      "explanation": "IN set membership test is to see whether a data value matches one of a list of target values. For instance, 'X IN (A, B, C)' is completely equivalent to '(X = A) OR (X = B) OR (X = C)'. In this example, customer name is tested with the customer names in 'depositor' table."
    },
    {
      "line_no": 4,
      "code": "          (SELECT customer_name ",
      "explanation": "SQL provides a mechanism for nesting subqueries. A subquery is a SELECT-FROM-WHERE statement or statement containing GROUP BY and HAVING that is nested within another query. <br>This query is to find customer name having account."
    },
    {
      "line_no": 5,
      "code": "           FROM depositor);",
      "explanation": ""
    }
  ],
